# Line endings are a CORRECTNESS control in this repo, not a formatting
# preference. Two product guarantees are byte-comparisons, so any per-platform
# byte difference breaks them silently rather than loudly:
#
#   * PROJECT_PLAN.md §7/§10 — "what you approved is byte-for-byte what gets
#     submitted". The resume renderer builds HTML from multi-line template
#     literals, so the SOURCE file's line endings are copied verbatim into the
#     rendered output. A CRLF checkout therefore changes the artifact itself,
#     not merely the file it was written in.
#   * Constraint #8 — tailored_hash is a sha256 over that rendered text and is
#     used as a cache key. A per-platform hash means the cache NEVER hits, and
#     the same resume is billed to Gemini twice.
#
# Why this was invisible until now: every machine is internally self-consistent,
# so every test passes everywhere in isolation. The divergence only exists
# BETWEEN a Windows contributor and the Ubuntu Actions runner, which is the one
# pair no single test run ever compares.
#
# Verified 2026-09-10, not assumed: core.autocrlf=true is set in this machine's
# SYSTEM gitconfig (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig), and cloning this repo
# into a temp directory produced CRLF working-tree files (PROJECT_PLAN.md 525 CR
# bytes, posted-at.ts 209, contract.ts 97) while the git blobs themselves are
# pure LF. The current working tree is LF only by accident — these files were
# authored here rather than checked out, and autocrlf only converts on checkout.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline for every path in the repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# text=auto lets git detect which files are text and normalise those to LF in
# the repository; eol=lf then forces LF into the WORKING TREE too, on every
# platform, overriding whatever core.autocrlf each contributor happens to have.
# Deliberately `text=auto` and not the blunter `text`: bare `text` asserts that
# EVERY file is text, which makes git rewrite bytes inside PNGs, PDFs and fonts
# and corrupt them. This repo renders resume PDFs, so that risk is real here.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files whose LF-ness is load-bearing, pinned explicitly.
# text=auto above uses a content heuristic; a file with an unlucky byte can be
# misdetected as binary and skip normalisation. These types are named so the
# heuristic cannot demote them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The renderer and its tests. Template literals here become product bytes.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf

# Migrations are applied by Postgres and diffed against a live schema; CRLF
# inside a dollar-quoted function body is stored as part of the function source.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Workflow files run on the Ubuntu runner. YAML is whitespace-significant and a
# stray CR can land inside a `run:` block and reach the shell as a literal \r.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Shell scripts break outright with CRLF: the interpreter reads `#!/bin/sh\r`
# and reports "not found" for a shell that plainly exists.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Config, docs and the lockfile — hashed, parsed or reviewed as diffs, and
# nothing here benefits from per-platform bytes.
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonc text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.npmrc text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files that must NOT be normalised.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Golden fixtures are compared byte for byte against a freshly rendered string,
# so they are the one place where conversion must be disabled outright rather
# than merely pinned. -text switches off ALL eol handling, so these survive even
# if the baseline rule above is ever loosened.
*.golden.html -text

# Batch files are the genuine exception: the Windows command interpreter needs
# CRLF, and an LF-only .cmd can execute only its first line.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary payloads. text=auto should already detect these, but naming them is
# cheap and a misdetected binary is corrupted silently and permanently.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
